#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kb-watch — the Watcher: currency checker for external/general facts. Detects; never edits a fact.

Phase 4 step 5 (§5A "Sources of truth -> fact-checkers"; the boxed "The Watcher is speculative —
scope it honestly"). The owner's decision for THIS step: a MINIMAL, OFFLINE Watcher. It compares a
saved snapshot against a recorded baseline, both already on disk. It never fetches anything itself —
live fetch is a later, isolated step (the plan's own SSRF/network-restricted-fetch scope). That is
why this script imports no network module: no urllib.request, no http.client, no socket, no requests.
Grep it if you doubt it.

WHY A SCRIPT, NOT AN AGENT (the plan's strongest defence against a spoofed page steering a KB
change): fetched/snapshotted text is never shown to a model by this tool. It is read as bytes,
stripped to plain text with the standard library only, matched against an operator-authored regex,
and the matched value is written to a queue entry as escaped, truncated DATA — never as an
instruction, never as prose composed from the page. There is no LLM in this loop.

THE ALLOWLIST (engine-side, never inside a library — kb/*): watcher/allowlist.json, a JSON list of
entries:

    {
      "source_id": "<stable id — should match the library source stub's slug for traceability>",
      "url": "<the source's URL, recorded on the drift signal only; NEVER fetched by this script>",
      "library": "<path to the library root that owns this source, e.g. kb/azure-devops>",
      "baseline_snapshot": "<path to the last-accepted snapshot — HTML or text>",
      "current_snapshot": "<path to the latest snapshot on disk — produced by a separate, later,
                            isolated fetch step; this script only ever reads it>",
      "fields": [
        {"name": "<field id>", "pattern": "<a Python regex with exactly one capture group,
                                             applied to the whitespace-normalised plain text>"}
      ]
    }

Relative paths ("library", "baseline_snapshot", "current_snapshot") resolve against the allowlist
file's own directory, so the allowlist travels with the engine regardless of cwd.

WHAT IT DOES, per entry:
  1. Read both snapshots. A missing, empty, or unreadable snapshot (baseline OR current) is an
     `unreachable` finding — NEVER silence, and NEVER folded into "unchanged" (§5A Cautions:
     "provenance rot" — unreachable is not the same claim as unchanged).
  2. Otherwise strip both to plain text (tags/scripts/styles removed, whitespace collapsed —
     standard library only: html.parser) and, for each declared field, extract the value from each
     side with its regex.
  3. old == new -> nothing (including "not found" on both sides). old != new -> a `drift` finding:
     exactly `field · old · new · source_url`, plus a stable id and today's date. No other text
     from the page is ever carried through.

THE QUEUE: reuses kb-audit's queue (`<library>/needs-attention.md`), its location convention (owned
by the library it describes, not the engine), its frontmatter template (SKILL.md step 3, copied
verbatim here), and its status vocabulary (open/acknowledged/resolved). kb-audit's SKILL.md does not
mandate a byte-for-byte item grammar (no example exists in this repository yet), so this script picks
one and states it plainly: a fenced ```yaml block of one-line records, the same convention the golden
set already uses (CONVENTIONS §10, `check-golden`) for machine-parseable, human-readable structure.
A future kb-audit sweep reads it the same way it reads its own findings.

STABLE ID: 8 hex chars of sha1(f"{category}|{source_id}|{field}|{new}") — the plan's own spec
("hash of source id + field + new value") plus `category` so a `drift` and an `unreachable` finding
for the same field can never collide. Re-running on an unchanged drift produces the same id and is
NOT re-added (dedupe). A value that changes again gets a new id and IS added, regardless of what
status a human left on the earlier entry for the old value — matching §5A: "an already-acknowledged
entry is not re-surfaced unless the source changes."

ESCAPING (§5A drift-signal schema; the injection defence): `old`/`new` are page-derived and
UNTRUSTED. Before they ever touch the queue file: embedded newlines/control characters are flattened
to spaces (so injected content can never start a fresh markdown line — no new heading, no new fence,
no frontmatter delimiter), a run of 3+ backticks is neutralised (cannot close/open a fence
mid-record), the value is truncated to 200 characters, and backslashes/double-quotes are escaped for
safe embedding in a double-quoted, single-line YAML-ish scalar. `source_id`/`field`/`source_url`/
`date` are operator-authored (from the allowlist, not the page) and are not treated as hostile, but
are still flattened to one line for hygiene.

DETECTION ONLY: this script writes ONLY `<library>/needs-attention.md` — never a fact, a stamp, a
source stub, or a library's INDEX.md (CONVENTIONS' router-line bookkeeping is deliberately left to a
human or to kb-audit; "writes only the queue" is read literally here). A library's queue is created
only when there is something to put in it (CONVENTIONS §3 / kb-audit SKILL.md step 3): a clean run
touches nothing.

DEFAULT IS WRITE, NOT --dry-run: the plan's three-band model (§5A "automate detection, gate
mutation") puts drift *detection* — and the queue it produces — in the fully-automated band; the
control is that the OUTPUT is a queue, never an edit, not that a human approves each write. This
matches kb-audit's own precedent exactly: it writes `needs-attention.md` unattended, with no
confirmation gate (documents/state-change-gates.md's entry for the kb-audit skill: "None —
justified... needs none to stay unattended-safe"). `--dry-run` is the explicit opt-out for a human
who wants to see what would be queued first.

Usage: kb-watch <allowlist.json> [--dry-run]
       kb-watch --dump <needs-attention.md>     # print every record this script can parse back out
                                                 # of a queue file, one per line (id, category,
                                                 # status, source_id, field, old, new, source_url,
                                                 # date), tab-separated — the exit criterion for
                                                 # "the queue still parses."
Exit 0 on a clean run (whether or not anything was queued). Exit 2 on a usage or config error
(bad JSON, a malformed entry, a library with no INDEX.md) — the run still processes every
well-formed entry first.
"""
import datetime, hashlib, html.parser, json, pathlib, re, sys

TODAY = datetime.date.today().isoformat()
MAX_LEN = 200
ID_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*-\s*id:\s*([0-9a-f]{8})\s*$")
KV_RE = re.compile(r'^\s{2}(\w+):\s*"?(.*?)"?\s*$')
FENCE_OPEN = "```yaml\n"
FENCE_CLOSE = "```\n"


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ plain-text stripping ----
class _TextStripper(html.parser.HTMLParser):
    """Standard library only. Drops <script>/<style> content; keeps everything else as text."""

    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__(convert_charrefs=True)
        self._skip = 0
        self.chunks = []

    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):
        if tag in ("script", "style"):
            self._skip += 1

    def handle_endtag(self, tag):
        if tag in ("script", "style") and self._skip:
            self._skip -= 1

    def handle_data(self, data):
        if not self._skip:
            self.chunks.append(data)


def strip_to_text(raw: str) -> str:
    p = _TextStripper()
    p.feed(raw)
    p.close()
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", "".join(p.chunks)).strip()


def read_snapshot(path: pathlib.Path):
    """(ok, text-or-reason). Missing/empty/unreadable is unreachable, never 'unchanged'."""
    if not path.is_file():
        return False, "snapshot file not found"
    try:
        raw = path.read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError as e:
        return False, f"snapshot unreadable: {e}"
    text = strip_to_text(raw)
    if not text:
        return False, "snapshot is empty after stripping to plain text"
    return True, text


def extract_field(text: str, pattern: str):
    m = re.search(pattern, text)
    if not m:
        return None
    return m.group(1) if m.lastindex else m.group(0)


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- escaping ----
def flatten(s: str) -> str:
    s = s.replace("\r\n", " ").replace("\r", " ").replace("\n", " ")
    s = re.sub(r"[\x00-\x08\x0b\x0c\x0e-\x1f]", " ", s)
    return s


def yaml_scalar(value, untrusted: bool) -> str:
    """A safe, single-line, double-quoted YAML-ish scalar. Untrusted values are additionally
    truncated and have fence-breaking backtick runs neutralised, per the drift-signal schema."""
    s = flatten("" if value is None else str(value))
    if untrusted:
        s = re.sub(r"`{3,}", "'''", s)
        if len(s) > MAX_LEN:
            s = s[:MAX_LEN] + " …[truncated]"
    s = s.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace('"', '\\"')
    return f'"{s}"'


def stable_id(category: str, source_id: str, field: str, new_value) -> str:
    raw = "" if new_value is None else str(new_value)
    payload = f"{category}|{source_id}|{field}|{raw}".encode()
    return hashlib.sha1(payload).hexdigest()[:8]


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the allowlist ----
def load_allowlist(path: pathlib.Path):
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:
        print(f"kb-watch: cannot read allowlist {path}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    if not isinstance(data, list):
        print(f"kb-watch: {path} must be a JSON list of entries", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    base = path.resolve().parent
    entries, errors = [], []
    for i, e in enumerate(data):
        where = f"entry {i} ({e.get('source_id', '?')!r})" if isinstance(e, dict) else f"entry {i}"
        if not isinstance(e, dict):
            errors.append(f"{where}: not an object"); continue
        missing = [k for k in ("source_id", "url", "library", "baseline_snapshot",
                                "current_snapshot", "fields") if k not in e]
        if missing:
            errors.append(f"{where}: missing field(s) {missing}"); continue
        fields = e["fields"]
        if not isinstance(fields, list) or not fields:
            errors.append(f"{where}: 'fields' must be a non-empty list"); continue
        bad_field = False
        for f in fields:
            if not isinstance(f, dict) or "name" not in f or "pattern" not in f:
                errors.append(f"{where}: each field needs 'name' and 'pattern'"); bad_field = True
                continue
            try:
                rx = re.compile(f["pattern"])
            except re.error as ex:
                errors.append(f"{where}: field {f.get('name')!r} bad pattern: {ex}"); bad_field = True
                continue
            if rx.groups < 1:
                errors.append(f"{where}: field {f.get('name')!r} pattern needs a capture group")
                bad_field = True
        if bad_field:
            continue

        def resolve(p):
            pp = pathlib.Path(p)
            return pp if pp.is_absolute() else (base / pp)

        entries.append({
            "source_id": e["source_id"], "url": e["url"],
            "library": resolve(e["library"]),
            "baseline_snapshot": resolve(e["baseline_snapshot"]),
            "current_snapshot": resolve(e["current_snapshot"]),
            "fields": fields,
        })
    return entries, errors


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the queue ----
def library_domain_scope(library_root: pathlib.Path):
    idx = library_root / "INDEX.md"
    if not idx.is_file():
        return None, None, f"{library_root} has no INDEX.md — not a library root"
    text = idx.read_text(errors="replace")
    d = re.search(r"^domain:\s*(.+)$", text, re.M)
    s = re.search(r"^scope:\s*(.+)$", text, re.M)
    if not d or not s:
        return None, None, f"{idx} has no domain:/scope: in its frontmatter"
    return d.group(1).strip(), s.group(1).strip(), None


def existing_ids(queue_path: pathlib.Path):
    if not queue_path.is_file():
        return set()
    return {m.group(1) for m in map(ID_RE.match, queue_path.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines())
            if m}


def render_record(rec: dict) -> str:
    lines = [f"- id: {rec['id']}"]
    for key in ("category", "status", "source_id", "field"):
        lines.append(f"  {key}: {yaml_scalar(rec[key], untrusted=False)}")
    lines.append(f"  old: {yaml_scalar(rec['old'], untrusted=True)}")
    lines.append(f"  new: {yaml_scalar(rec['new'], untrusted=True)}")
    lines.append(f"  source_url: {yaml_scalar(rec['source_url'], untrusted=False)}")
    lines.append(f"  date: {yaml_scalar(rec['date'], untrusted=False)}")
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n\n"


def new_queue_file(domain: str, scope: str) -> str:
    fm = (
        "---\n"
        "name: needs-attention\n"
        "description: Open findings from automated drift detection (kb-watch) and hygiene sweeps "
        "(kb-audit), by severity.\n"
        "memory_type: reference\n"
        f"domain: {domain}\n"
        f"scope: {scope}\n"
        "metadata:\n"
        "  type: index\n"
        "  node_type: memory\n"
        f"  created: {TODAY}\n"
        "tags: [meta, hygiene, queue, watcher]\n"
        "keywords: [needs attention, queue, findings, drift, watcher, unreachable]\n"
        "---\n"
        "# Needs attention\n\n"
        "Findings from automated drift checks and hygiene sweeps. Detection only — nothing here "
        "was fixed automatically. See CONVENTIONS.md §3 and "
        "plugins/knowledge-tools/skills/kb-audit/SKILL.md for the queue's conventions.\n\n"
    )
    return fm + FENCE_OPEN + FENCE_CLOSE


def append_records(queue_path: pathlib.Path, records: list):
    body = "".join(render_record(r) for r in records)
    if not queue_path.is_file():
        domain, scope, err = library_domain_scope(queue_path.parent)
        if err:
            return err
        queue_path.write_text(new_queue_file(domain, scope)[:-len(FENCE_CLOSE)] + body + FENCE_CLOSE)
        return None
    text = queue_path.read_text(errors="replace")
    spans = list(re.finditer(r"```yaml\n(?:.*?\n)?```\n", text, re.S))
    if spans:
        last = spans[-1]
        insert_at = last.end() - len(FENCE_CLOSE)
        text = text[:insert_at] + body + text[insert_at:]
    else:
        text = text.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n" + FENCE_OPEN + body + FENCE_CLOSE
    queue_path.write_text(text)
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- record parse ----
def parse_records(text: str):
    """The same shape this script writes: '- id: x' opens a record, two-space-indented 'key:
    value' lines fill it in. Mirrors check-golden's parser style (line-based, no yaml library)."""
    records, cur = [], None
    for raw in text.splitlines():
        m = ID_RE.match(raw)
        if m:
            if cur:
                records.append(cur)
            cur = {"id": m.group(1)}
            continue
        if cur is not None:
            kv = KV_RE.match(raw)
            if kv:
                cur[kv.group(1)] = re.sub(r"\\(.)", r"\1", kv.group(2))
            elif raw.strip() == "" or raw.strip().startswith("```"):
                pass
            elif not raw.startswith("  "):
                records.append(cur); cur = None
    if cur:
        records.append(cur)
    return records


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ main ----
def process_entry(entry: dict, dry_run: bool):
    """Returns (findings_written_or_would_write, errors) for this one source."""
    source_id, url, library = entry["source_id"], entry["url"], entry["library"]
    findings, errors = [], []

    ok_b, base_txt = read_snapshot(entry["baseline_snapshot"])
    ok_c, cur_txt = read_snapshot(entry["current_snapshot"])
    if not (ok_b and ok_c):
        reason = base_txt if not ok_b else cur_txt
        findings.append({"category": "unreachable", "status": "open", "source_id": source_id,
                          "field": "-", "old": None, "new": reason, "source_url": url, "date": TODAY})
    else:
        for f in entry["fields"]:
            old = extract_field(base_txt, f["pattern"])
            new = extract_field(cur_txt, f["pattern"])
            if old == new:
                continue
            findings.append({"category": "drift", "status": "open", "source_id": source_id,
                              "field": f["name"],
                              "old": old if old is not None else "(not found)",
                              "new": new if new is not None else "(not found)",
                              "source_url": url, "date": TODAY})

    for finding in findings:
        finding["id"] = stable_id(finding["category"], finding["source_id"], finding["field"],
                                   finding["new"])

    queue_path = library / "needs-attention.md"
    seen = existing_ids(queue_path)
    to_write = [f for f in findings if f["id"] not in seen]
    skipped = len(findings) - len(to_write)

    print(f"kb-watch: {source_id}: {len(findings)} finding(s), {skipped} already queued, "
          f"{len(to_write)} new")
    for f in to_write:
        tag = "WOULD QUEUE" if dry_run else "queued"
        print(f"  {tag} [{f['category']}] {f['id']}  {f['field']}: "
              f"{f['old']!r} -> {f['new']!r}  ({queue_path})")

    if to_write and not dry_run:
        err = append_records(queue_path, to_write)
        if err:
            errors.append(f"{source_id}: {err}")
    return to_write, errors


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["--dump"]:
        if len(argv) != 2:
            print("usage: kb-watch --dump <needs-attention.md>", file=sys.stderr); return 2
        qp = pathlib.Path(argv[1])
        if not qp.is_file():
            print(f"kb-watch: {qp} does not exist", file=sys.stderr); return 2
        for r in parse_records(qp.read_text(errors="replace")):
            cols = [r.get(k, "") for k in
                    ("id", "category", "status", "source_id", "field", "old", "new",
                     "source_url", "date")]
            print("\t".join(cols))
        return 0

    args = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("--")]
    flags = {a for a in argv if a.startswith("--")}
    if len(args) != 1 or flags - {"--dry-run"}:
        print(__doc__.strip().split("\n\n")[-1], file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    dry_run = "--dry-run" in flags

    entries, config_errors = load_allowlist(pathlib.Path(args[0]))
    for e in config_errors:
        print(f"kb-watch: CONFIG ERROR: {e}", file=sys.stderr)

    if not entries:
        print("kb-watch: no watchable sources configured" +
              (" (allowlist is empty)" if not config_errors else ""))
        return 2 if config_errors else 0

    run_errors = []
    total_new = 0
    for entry in entries:
        written, errors = process_entry(entry, dry_run)
        total_new += len(written)
        run_errors += errors

    for e in run_errors:
        print(f"kb-watch: ERROR: {e}", file=sys.stderr)

    print(f"\nkb-watch: {len(entries)} source(s) checked, {total_new} new "
          f"{'would-queue' if dry_run else 'queued'} finding(s)")
    return 2 if (config_errors or run_errors) else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
